eason":2018,"team":"TEN","distance":0.6803,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Commanders went 7-9 in 2018 and were outscored 359 to 281. The defense did most of the work, finishing 17th in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 26th in EPA per play. What set them apart was the 2nd most linebackers on the field in the league.","offense":"The Commanders offense was well below average by EPA per play, and it was much better running the ball than passing it. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Alex Smith ran the passing game, with Jordan Reed leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Commanders defense was about average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was sack rate, where it ranked 5th, and its weakest was EPA allowed per rush, where it ranked 25th. Ryan Kerrigan led the team with 13 sacks, and Matthew Ioannidis added 7.5 despite playing only 49% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had the 2nd most linebackers on the field in the league.","special":"The Commanders were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 90% of field goals, which was well above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into tight ends, about 8% of the money on the books. Their quarterbacks were among the oldest in the league.","players":"Alex Smith played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 2,180 yards. Targets were spread around, with Jordan Reed and Josh Doctson leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. Adrian Peterson handled most of the work in the backfield with 75% of the carries. On defense, Ryan Kerrigan led the team with 13 sacks and D.J. Swearinger picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2021 Bears and the 2019 Colts under Frank Reich. Their defense looked most like the 2023 Packers and the 2019 Bears.","trends":"2018 is the first season covered here, so there is no earlier year to compare it with. The charts below show how the Commanders changed over the seasons that followed.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2018. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}